Material inside it that belongs to others keeps its own terms:
  - Places, population figures, depopulation and founding dates, 1940s village land
    boundaries and the list of oral-history interviews: Palestine Open Maps
    (Visualizing Palestine, palopenmaps.org; github PalOpenMaps/pom-data), drawing on
    the British censuses of 1922 and 1931 and the Village Statistics of 1945, Zochrot,
    Palestine Remembered and the work of Salman Abu Sitta. No licence is stated for
    this data; it is credited to its makers and used with acknowledgement.
  - Historical maps: Survey of Palestine 1:250,000 (1946) and 1:20,000 (1940s),
    public domain, shown live from palopenmaps.org.
  - Air photographs: Royal Air Force, 1940s, as compiled by Palestine Open Maps
    (shown live via jsDelivr).
  - Satellite picture: Esri World Imagery, shown live under Esri's terms of use;
    not stored in this repository.
  - Outlines: OCHA Common Operational Datasets (HDX, CC BY-IGO); geoBoundaries
    (from OpenStreetMap, ODbL 1.0); Natural Earth (public domain).
  - The 1937 and 1947 partition plans are traced from the Royal Commission's map
    (UK Government, public domain) and UN Map No. 103.1(b) (United Nations).
  - Moments are written for this page from Wikipedia articles (CC BY-SA), which
    they link; no Wikipedia text is reproduced beyond short quotations.
